WEBVTT

NOTE This transcript is the episode script as written and fact-checked, timed to the recording. Timestamps are approximate within a sentence.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.600
What's up guys, welcome back to CrimeTimeSnacks.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:16.600
On Monday, September 21, 2026, at ten in the morning, three judges of the Indiana Court of Appeals are scheduled to sit in the Supreme Court Courtroom at the Indiana Statehouse.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.200
Each side gets thirty minutes.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.900
The question in front of them is whether Richard Allen got a fair trial.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.100
If you listened to my Delphi episode back in 2022, you know where we left it.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.700
Two girls, a bridge, a few seconds of video, and no arrest.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.500
A lot has happened since then.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:33.800
There was an arrest.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:34.900
There was a trial.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.900
There was a verdict, and a sentence of 130 years.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.600
So let me be clear about where things stand before we start.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.000
Richard Allen is not an accused man anymore.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.400
A jury convicted him of murdering Abby Williams and Libby German.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.100
He is also a man with an appeal pending, and he has the right to make it.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.800
An appeal is not proof that the jury got it wrong, and it is not proof that they got it right.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.000
It is a set of questions.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.500
Today I want to walk you through how this case got to a verdict, and then through the three questions his lawyers are asking.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:04.400
Let's unpack it.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.400
I am going to keep the recap short, because most of you know it.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:17.700
At one thirty in the afternoon on February 13, 2017, Libby German's older sister, Kelsi, dropped two girls off on County Road 300 North, outside Delphi, Indiana.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:18.800
Libby was fourteen.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.200
Her friend, Abby Williams, was thirteen.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:28.300
They had the day off from school, and they were going to walk the Monon High Bridge, an abandoned rail bridge over Deer Creek.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.800
At 2:07, Libby posted a photo of Abby walking the bridge.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.100
After that, nobody heard from them again.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.800
They were supposed to meet Libby's father at 3:15.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:37.900
They did not show up.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:42.600
The families searched on their own first, and at 5:30 the girls were reported missing.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.900
Around noon the next day, Valentine's Day, their bodies were found on the north bank of Deer Creek.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.600
What made this case different from the start was Libby's phone.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.100
When a man came up behind them on that bridge, she started recording.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:02:03.200
Two days after the murders, Indiana State Police were already circulating a grainy still image of a man walking the rail bridge, hands in his pockets, head down.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.100
People started calling him Bridge Guy.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:08.600
On February 22, police released a piece of audio from the same phone.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.100
It is a man's voice, muffled, saying three words.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:11.800
Down the hill.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.600
Police called Libby a hero for having the presence of mind to record it.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:21.200
They also said there was more on the phone, and that they were holding it back so they would not compromise a future trial.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.400
At that point the reward stood at 41,000 dollars.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.100
Then the years went by.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.000
A composite sketch came out that July.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:32.000
In April 2019, State Police announced what they called a new direction and released a second sketch.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:40.600
They told the public the killer might be hiding in plain sight, and that he was almost certainly familiar with Delphi, from living there, working there, or some other reason.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.000
They were right about that.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.300
They just did not know they already had his name.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.700
In that same 2019 announcement, police asked for help with one more thing.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:58.300
They wanted to identify the driver of a vehicle that had been left at the former Child Services office, just off the Hoosier Heartland Highway, between noon and five on the day of the murders.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.400
Over the years, investigators ran down thousands of leads.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.000
None of them ended in an arrest.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:08.800
Three days after the murders, a man walked up to an Indiana conservation officer who was helping on the case.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.900
They talked in a grocery store parking lot.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:16.900
The man said he had been on the trails the afternoon the girls disappeared, and that he had passed three girls along the way.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.200
His name was Richard Allen.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:21.300
The officer typed up his notes and turned them in.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.100
And then the paperwork went nowhere.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:27.600
Allen's last name was recorded incorrectly on the tip, and the file was marked cleared.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.200
It sat like that for five years.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:35.800
In September 2022, a volunteer file clerk named Kathy Shank was helping investigators organize thousands of old tips.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:42.800
She found the conservation officer's notes, worked out that the tip was about Richard Allen, and brought it to the investigator on the case.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.000
At trial, Carroll County Sheriff Tony Liggett put it this way.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.500
The tip fell in the cracks, he said, and Allen was hiding in plain sight.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.700
Things moved fast after that.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:54.500
On October 13, 2022, police interviewed Allen again.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:04:00.500
He told them he had worn jeans and a black or blue jacket that day, and that he had gone out to the bridge to watch fish.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:03.000
Police searched his home and recovered a pistol.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.900
Remember that pistol, because it becomes the center of the physical case.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.700
On October 26, 2022, Richard Allen was taken into custody.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:15.400
On October 31, State Police announced he had been charged with two counts of murder.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:16.600
He pleaded not guilty.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:23.100
Time magazine called it the first major break in a case that had captivated national attention for nearly six years.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.600
A month later, Judge Frances Gull unsealed the probable cause affidavit, with redactions.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:37.800
According to that document, the video from Libby's phone showed one of the girls mentioning a gun as a man in a dark jacket and jeans approached and ordered them down the hill.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:44.200
Investigators had found an unspent forty caliber round less than two feet from one of the bodies, between the two girls.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:49.900
One witness described a man walking away from the bridge in a blue jacket and blue jeans, muddy and bloody.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:53.600
Another described a car parked oddly, as if to hide its license plate.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.400
Investigators said the description matched a car Allen owned in 2017.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:58.600
So who was he?

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:03.000
Richard Matthew Allen was born in September 1972 and grew up in Mexico, Indiana.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:12.000
He played football and ran track in high school, graduated in 1991, studied accounting at a community college, and had short stints in the Army and the National Guard.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.700
He married and had one daughter.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:17.300
From 2003 to 2013 he managed a Walmart in Logansport.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.400
In December 2006 he moved to Delphi.

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:25.200
After Walmart, he went to work for CVS, and he got his pharmacy technician license in February 2018.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.200
That is one year after the murders.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:29.800
Delphi is a town of about 3,000 people.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.500
One resident told A&amp;E that he was a familiar face if you went into the CVS.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:37.600
And there is one detail that people in town could not get past after the arrest.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:44.400
Libby's grandmother, Becky Patty, told reporters that Allen had printed photos of the two girls, at no charge, for their funerals.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.600
Years later, in court, she said it this way.

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:49.600
He developed photos with no remorse, and he didn't blink.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:52.400
Allen never hid the fact that he was out there.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.200
Shortly after the murders he told his wife he had been on the trail that day.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:07.800
After his arrest, he told investigators he had been at his mother's house in Peru, Indiana, that morning, got to the trail around midday, saw three girls pass by, saw nobody else, and watched a stock ticker on his phone while he walked.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:10.600
His defense would later point at all of this.

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:13.600
He lived in Delphi for more than five years after the killings.

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:14.900
He went to police on his own.

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:21.300
The prosecution pointed at the same facts and saw something else: a man who put himself on that trail at the time the girls disappeared.

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:24.800
The trial was originally set for March 20, 2023.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:29.200
It did not start until October 2024, and the road in between was rough.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:32.500
In December 2022, Judge Gull put a gag order on the case.

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:39.300
The defense asked to move the trial out of Carroll County, arguing that the extensive media attention made a fair local jury impossible.

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:47.900
Two public defenders, Brad Rozzi and Andrew Baldwin, were appointed to represent Allen, and the first trial date was pushed back so they could get through the discovery.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.400
Then, in October 2023, crime scene photos leaked from the defense team's office.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.000
Judge Gull removed both attorneys from the case, citing gross negligence.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:00.300
The attorneys took that to the Indiana Supreme Court, and the justices reinstated them.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.100
Those photos never went away, either.

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:10.000
After sentencing, the prosecutor said they were still out there, and he asked anyone who receives them to delete them rather than put the families through it again.

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:13.400
The other fight was over what the defense wanted to argue.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:20.000
Allen's lawyers suggested the girls were killed as part of a pagan ritual sacrifice, by followers of Odinism, a Norse pagan religion.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:29.800
They accused police of ignoring evidence from the crime scene, and they pointed to a search warrant request from March 2017 in which an FBI agent said the bodies appeared to have been moved and staged.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:33.800
Judge Gull repeatedly rejected motions to bring that theory in front of the jury.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:35.000
Hold on to that ruling.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.400
It is one of the three things the Court of Appeals is being asked about.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:42.500
While all of that was going on, Richard Allen was not sitting in a county jail.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:50.400
He was being held at Westville Correctional Facility, a state prison, and his lawyers say he spent thirteen months in solitary confinement before he got his day in court.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.200
Here is what the jury heard about that time.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:56.000
Monica Wala was the lead psychologist at Westville.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:59.000
She testified that Allen first told her he was innocent.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:03.000
In April 2023, around the time he was put back on suicide watch, that changed.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.600
He was on suicide watch twice.

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:13.900
According to her testimony, he showed bizarre behavior, including eating his own feces and banging his head, and at one point he was diagnosed with a brief psychotic disorder.

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:15.800
And he started confessing.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:20.000
On April 3, 2023, on a recorded call to his wife, Kathy, he said, I killed Abby and Libby.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.000
When she pushed back, he said, I think I did.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:22.700
I don't know.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:25.600
I wish they would kill me and let me apologize to the families.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:31.600
On a call with his mother the following month he said, I'm just worried you guys aren't going to love me because I said I did it.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:34.900
Wala testified that Allen told her, I killed Abby and Libby.

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:35.400
I'm sorry.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:43.600
She said he told her he had originally planned to sexually assault the girls, that he ran when he saw a van nearby, and that he cut their throats and covered their bodies with sticks.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:47.100
Prosecutors counted more than sixty of these statements.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:47.700
To his wife.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:48.400
To his mother.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:52.400
To the psychologist, the warden, other prison employees, and other inmates.

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:54.400
In person, on the phone, and in writing.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:09:01.500
The defense called Deanna Dwenger, a clinical psychologist who worked for the Department of Correction's behavioral health service.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:11.400
She testified that in April 2023, the same month the confessions began, Allen was diagnosed with a serious mental illness, and that a team of mental health professionals concluded he had a grave disability.

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:15.400
A neuropsychologist hired by the defense testified to pretty severe depression.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:17.700
So both sides agree that he said it.

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:22.200
What they disagree about is what state he was in when he said it, and whose fault that was.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:24.300
That is the second question in the appeal.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:30.400
The trial began on October 18, 2024, in Delphi, in front of a sequestered jury of seven women and five men.

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:32.600
There were seventeen days of testimony.

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:41.700
People were so desperate to get in that, according to one resident, podcasters and YouTubers were paying people to sit in lawn chairs overnight to hold their place in line.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:43.500
The first day set the tone.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:49.100
Becky Patty, Libby's grandmother, took the stand and described her last conversation with her granddaughter.

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:50.800
She had told Libby to take a jacket.

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:52.700
Libby answered, Grandma, I'll be OK.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:55.600
Prosecutor Nick McLeland's theory was simple.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:57.000
Richard Allen is Bridge Guy.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:00.200
He forced the girls down the hill at gunpoint, and he killed them.

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:03.100
Prosecutors told the jury both girls had their throats cut.

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:05.700
Abby was found fully clothed, in Libby's clothing.

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:06.700
Libby was found nude.

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:13.300
The phone was found underneath Abby's body, and McLeland later praised Abby for hiding it from the killer so that police could find it.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:17.900
The state showed jurors a digitally enhanced, 43-second version of Libby's video.

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:27.500
State Police Master Trooper Brian Harshman testified that he had listened to more than 700 of Allen's prison phone calls, and that in his opinion the voice of Bridge Guy is the voice of Richard Allen.

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:32.000
Witnesses said they saw a man on the trail that day whose clothes matched the man in the video.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:35.600
Investigators testified that Allen was off work that day and had no alibi.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:37.100
Then the round.

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:49.000
A State Police firearms examiner testified that the unspent forty caliber cartridge found between the girls had been cycled through Allen's pistol, a Sig Sauer P226, based on the quality and quantity of the marks on it.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:51.900
That was the state's key piece of physical evidence.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:56.600
And the jury heard the confessions, including the phone calls, in Allen's own voice.

00:10:56.600 --> 00:11:02.000
A journalist who sat through the trial, Susan Hendricks, said he did not sound psychotic or manic on those calls.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:04.300
She said it sounded like a casual conversation.

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:08.900
Brad Rozzi's closing argument for the defense came down to three things.

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:09.900
A broken timeline.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:11.000
False confessions.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:12.300
And no DNA and no weapon.

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:17.900
On the physical evidence, the defense had a real point, and the state's own witness made it for them.

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:22.300
A DNA expert testifying for the prosecution found none of Allen's DNA at the crime scene.

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:26.100
None of Abby's or Libby's DNA was found on anything recovered from his home.

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:27.900
The murder weapon was never found.

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:36.400
On the round, the defense asked why more images had not been taken of the cartridge, and suggested it could have come from a law enforcement officer's weapon.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:44.100
On identification, Rozzi told jurors that no witness clearly identified Allen as the man on the trail or on the bridge that afternoon.

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:51.200
And on the confessions, the defense said they were false, the product of months in solitary confinement while his mental health fell apart.

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:56.500
Their argument was that you cannot take the words of a man in that condition and treat them like a calm admission.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:59.900
Rozzi closed with the fact that Allen never left town.

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:04.200
He had every chance to run, he told the jury, but he did not, because he didn't do it.

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:10.200
Nick McLeland had already walked the jury through the evidence, the photos, the video, and the recorded calls.

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:16.200
He told them he believed the evidence was firmly convincing that Richard Allen is Bridge Guy, and he killed Abby and Libby.

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:18.800
The jury got the case on Thursday, November 7.

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:23.100
Before the verdict came in, Judge Gull said something from the bench that turned out to be true.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:26.700
Regardless of what the verdict is, she said, people aren't going to be happy.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:30.300
They deliberated for about nineteen hours over three days.

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:39.900
On November 11, 2024, they found Richard Allen guilty on all four counts: two counts of murder and two counts of felony murder, for killing each girl while attempting to commit kidnapping.

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:42.400
Allen sat without emotion as it was read.

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:45.100
Outside the courthouse the crowd broke into applause.

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:48.000
Some people out there were holding signs supporting him.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:51.400
His wife, Kathy, told a reporter on her way out, this isn't over at all.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.000
Sentencing was December 20, 2024.

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.900
The seats reserved for Allen's family were empty.

00:12:56.900 --> 00:12:58.600
None of his relatives came.

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:07.700
Judge Gull gave him sixty-five years for each murder, to be served one after the other, 130 years in total, with credit for the 760 days he had already served.

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:12.200
The gag order came off that day, and for the first time the families could talk.

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:17.300
Becky Patty told the court, I can never change my choice to let Libby and Abby go to the trails that day.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:26.900
Abby's grandmother, Diane Erskin, said she never let herself leave the courtroom, even during the worst testimony, because Abby's last words on the recording were, don't leave me up here.

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:30.700
Libby's grandfather, Mike Patty, spoke directly to Allen.

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:33.100
You need to stand up, he said, and not appeal.

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:41.300
And Judge Gull told him, I've spent 27 years as a judge, and you rank right up there with the most heinous crimes in the state of Indiana.

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:45.000
These families, she said, will deal with your carnage forever.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:52.000
State Police Lieutenant Jerry Holeman told the judge the girls had been stalked, kidnapped, humiliated, and treated like animals.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:56.000
He called Allen manipulative and persuasive, and said he showed a lack of remorse.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:00.500
Libby's mother, Carrie Timmons, said Allen's decisions had created a path of destruction.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:05.000
Afterward, Sheriff Liggett apologized to the families for how long it had taken.

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:07.600
There will never be any closure in this case, he said.

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:11.000
A form of justice was served, but it does not bring Abby or Libby back.

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:17.300
And under Indiana law, a person convicted of more than one murder who receives a sentence like this one is not eligible for parole.

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:20.100
Unless a court steps in, Richard Allen will die in prison.

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:21.300
He appealed.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:25.300
Allen went first to Westville and then to Pendleton Correctional Facility.

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:33.000
On July 18, 2025, Indiana moved him out of state, to the Lexington Assessment and Reception Center, a maximum-security prison in Lexington, Oklahoma.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:36.400
The Department of Correction described the move as a precaution.

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:45.300
That April, the interrogation videos and the recorded confession calls that had been played at trial were released to the public, so anyone can now hear what the jury heard.

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:49.700
His appellate lawyers asked for more time more than once.

00:14:49.700 --> 00:14:53.800
On December 17, 2025, they filed their brief, 113 pages long.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:58.400
The Indiana Attorney General's office answered on March 25, 2026.

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:05.200
The defense filed a reply on April 27, along with a motion asking the judges to hear the case in person.

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:07.200
On May 14, the court said yes.

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:11.600
The panel is Judges Nancy Vaidik, Elaine Brown, and Robert Altice.

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:16.600
The record in front of them is 22 volumes of trial transcript and 15 volumes of exhibits.

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:18.600
And the brief raises three issues.

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:24.100
I will take them one at a time, with the state's answer to each, because you deserve to hear both.

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:27.600
The first issue is the search of Allen's home in October 2022.

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:31.500
That search produced the pistol, and the pistol produced the match to the round.

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:34.900
If the search falls, the state's key physical evidence is in trouble.

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:40.500
Allen's lawyers say the document police used to get that warrant mischaracterized what witnesses had said.

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:50.300
Their claim is that law enforcement omitted and altered witness descriptions to make Allen seem like the man in the Bridge Guy video, and that the trial court should never have admitted what came out of the house.

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:55.100
The state's answer is that the warrant was obtained properly and that it contained no false statements.

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:58.800
The second issue is the one that could matter beyond this case.

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:01.800
The defense argues that the confessions were involuntary.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:10.000
They say Allen was deeply ill, held in what they call unprecedented confinement, and that what he said in that condition was the result of psychological coercion.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:17.600
They argue that using those statements violated federal due process, and also a protection in Indiana's own constitution against unnecessary rigor.

00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:25.400
In their request for oral argument, his lawyers called this an issue of first impression, meaning a question the court has not squarely answered before.

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:30.700
Can statements made during a psychosis, caused by prolonged solitary confinement, be considered voluntary?

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:33.000
The state says yes, in this case.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:36.700
The Attorney General's office argues that nobody coerced anything.

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:43.400
In its telling, the sixty-plus confessions were voluntary, they were unprompted, and they were unrelated to his psychosis.

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:46.400
The third issue is about what the jury never heard.

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:52.300
Allen's lawyers argue he was denied his right to present a complete defense, and they tie that to the Fourteenth Amendment.

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:57.000
They wanted to tell the jury that Odinists committed these murders as a ritual killing.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.100
They wanted to offer alternative suspects.

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:04.000
They wanted to show the jury one of the early police sketches, which they say looks nothing like Allen.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:05.600
The trial court kept all of it out.

00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:13.000
They also say the court let the prosecution use inadmissible hearsay while blocking the defense from showing the full circumstances of the confessions.

00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:16.300
The state's answer is that the judge ruled correctly.

00:17:16.300 --> 00:17:22.900
It says the defense wanted to point at two third-party suspects but offered no evidence putting either of them at the crime scene.

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:28.700
It says the opinion of the defense's ritual expert, Dawn Perlmutter, was formed before she had seen any evidence.

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:35.200
On the ritual theory itself, the state wrote, it was a speculative motive, and there is no history of human sacrifice in Odinism.

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:44.000
One local station added that police did look into the Odinism angle early on, with input from the FBI's behavioral analysis unit, and never found evidence to take it further.

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:47.100
And the state comes back to the fact it started with.

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:52.700
Whatever else is argued, Richard Allen put himself on the Monon High Bridge trail at the time the girls disappeared.

00:17:53.300 --> 00:17:54.600
So what should you expect?

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:55.300
Not a verdict.

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:59.900
Monday is thirty minutes a side, in front of three judges who have had the briefs for months.

00:17:59.900 --> 00:18:02.400
The Court of Appeals will rule later, in writing.

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:04.600
Allen's lawyers are asking for a new trial.

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:08.300
The state is asking the court to leave the conviction exactly where it is.

00:18:08.900 --> 00:18:12.700
One legal analyst quoted by CBS after the sentencing put the stakes plainly.

00:18:12.700 --> 00:18:18.400
The conviction could be reversed, he said, if an appeal finds that the jury did not hear a fair amount of the evidence.

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:23.400
That is the third question, and it is why the defense has spent so much of its brief on what was kept out.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:26.100
I will tell you how I am thinking about it.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:29.000
Each of these three questions is a real legal question.

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:31.600
How honest does a warrant application have to be?

00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:34.900
When is a confession from a sick man in an isolation cell his own?

00:18:34.900 --> 00:18:38.100
How much room does a defendant get to point at somebody else?

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:40.600
Courts take those seriously, and they should.

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:43.600
But none of them is the question the jury answered.

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:50.400
Twelve people sat through seventeen days of testimony, heard that voice on the phone calls, watched that video, and said guilty four times.

00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:55.600
An appeals court asking whether the trial was fair is not the same as an appeals court saying he did not do it.

00:18:55.600 --> 00:19:00.300
If you see a headline on Monday that treats it that way, in either direction, be careful with it.

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:03.200
Whatever the court decides, I will come back to it.

00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:06.500
I want to end where this case should always end, with the two of them.

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:14.600
In 2017, Libby's mother asked people to put orange lights on their porches, for the girls, and as a reminder that whoever did this was still out there.

00:19:14.600 --> 00:19:16.800
Homeowners across central Indiana did it.

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:21.100
That same year, the families announced they would build a park in the girls' memory.

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:24.500
Today there is an Abby and Libby Memorial Park a mile north of Delphi.

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:30.300
The library at Delphi Community Middle School, where both girls went, is now the Abby and Libby Memorial Library.

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:37.800
At sentencing, Libby's mother said her daughter would be twenty-two now, and that the family has stopped celebrating her birthday because it is too hard.

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:41.400
Abby's grandmother said she has watched Abby's friends graduate from college.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.700
And the prosecutor said the thing I keep coming back to.

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:48.700
Libby had the wherewithal to pull out her phone, to know something wasn't right.

00:19:48.700 --> 00:19:52.300
He called that recording arguably the biggest piece of evidence they had.

00:19:52.300 --> 00:19:55.400
A fourteen-year-old did that, in the last minutes of her life.

00:19:55.900 --> 00:19:58.500
The briefs, the rulings and the trial videos are public.

00:19:58.500 --> 00:19:59.100
Read the file.

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:00.300
Form your own conclusion.

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:01.300
That's it for this one.

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:02.700
Thanks for hanging out with me.

00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:05.500
This has been CrimeTimeSnacks, and I'll catch you next time.
